# stm32f407 — an STMicroelectronics STM32F407VGT6.
#
#   rsemu run stm32f407 --media firmware=blink.bin
#   rsemu run stm32f407 -p console=b --media firmware=blink.bin
#
# A real part, not a synthesis: LQFP100, a Cortex-M4 at 168 MHz, 1 MiB of flash,
# 112 + 16 KiB of SRAM plus 64 KiB of core-coupled memory, and the ports that
# package actually bonds. It is the microcontroller on ST's own STM32F4
# Discovery board, which is why PD12–PD15 below are described as LEDs and why
# USART2 is on PA2/PA3.
#
# Sources: ST **RM0090** rev 21 (reference manual for STM32F405/415, F407/417,
# F427/437 and F429/439) — §2.3 for the memory map, §7 for the reset and clock
# tree, §8 for the GPIOs, §30 for the USART, and Table 62 for the vector table.
# ST **DS8626** rev 9 (the F407VG datasheet) for what the LQFP100 bonds and for
# the alternate-function table. The ARMv7-M Architecture Reference Manual, ARM
# DDI 0403, for the core. No emulator source of any licence was consulted
# (ROADMAP.md §1).
#
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# How a peripheral raises an interrupt here
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
#
# **The NVIC is inside the core.** On every other board in this tree the CPU has
# one or two interrupt inputs and a separate controller — a PLIC, an 8259A —
# decides which source drove them. An M-profile core has no such thing to wire:
# the NVIC's enable, pending and priority registers are part of the core's own
# system block at 0xE000E000, and interrupt *n* is exception number *n + 16*.
#
# So a peripheral drives the core's pin `irq{n}` directly, and the number is
# written **here**:
#
#   wire usart2.irq -> cpu.irq38
#
# 38 is USART2's position in RM0090 Table 62. That number is a fact about this
# *part*, not about the ARMv7-M architecture and not about a USART — a USART on
# an H7 is a different number and the same device model — so it belongs in the
# board file where the part is chosen, and a device model must never contain
# one. `cpu.nmi` and `cpu.reset` are the two inputs that are not numbered.
#
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# What this board does not have
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
#
# **RCC is a device, and the polls it answers terminate.** `rcc` below is
# `st.rcc` at the real address. Setting `CR.HSEON` produces `CR.HSERDY` after
# `ready-delay` ticks of the device's own clock domain and `CR.PLLON` produces
# `CR.PLLRDY` the same way; `CFGR.SW` is refused outright while the source it
# names is not ready, so `SWS` agrees only once the switch has actually
# happened. The three spin loops every vendor `SystemInit` opens with therefore
# exit, which is exactly what the scratch RAM window that used to be here could
# not do — RAM reads back what was written and all three of those are
# hardware-set bits.
#
# Two things it still does not do, both visible rather than hidden:
#
# * **It re-rates nothing.** The oscillator ratios below are fixed at the
#   values a configured F407 runs at, so a guest that reprograms `PLLCFGR`
#   changes what `st.rcc` computes and publishes and changes no scheduler rate.
#   `core::clock` can re-rate a domain; no seam reaches it from a device yet
#   (`src/dev/stm32/rcc.rs`, "The clock outputs").
# * **Nothing consumes the gates.** Every `xxENR` and `xxRSTR` bit is a real
#   output pin, and no peripheral in this tree has an enable input to wire it
#   to — which is why there is not one `wire rcc.apb1en...` line below. A
#   peripheral whose clock the guest never enables still works here.
#
# What is genuinely absent is the rest of the die: SPI, I2C2 and I2C3, the OTG
# cores, SDIO, the ADCs, the DAC, CAN, the RTC, the FSMC and Ethernet. They are
# separate device classes and separate `object` lines, and this file is the
# floor they get added to.
#
# GPIOF, GPIOG and GPIOI exist on the die but the LQFP100 bonds no pins of them,
# so this board does not instantiate them. A file for an F407ZGT6 (LQFP144, A–G)
# or an F407IGH6 (UFBGA176, A–I) copies the `gpioe` block and changes two
# numbers.

machine "stm32f407" {
  # Which character port USART2's console attaches to, so two of these can run
  # in one process without typing at each other (`-p console=b`).
  param console = "console"

  # Which modelling style the I2C link uses, and which named bus it meets its
  # devices on. `docs/buses/low-speed.md` asks for the first of these not to be
  # made by accident, so `st.i2c` refuses to default it and this file chooses:
  #
  #   i2clink = "wired"          the default. SCL and SDA are two real
  #                              open-drain nets, wired-AND across every driver,
  #                              one edge per half bit period on the scheduler.
  #                              Clock stretching and arbitration are levels on
  #                              those nets rather than flags, and anything else
  #                              on the board can watch them.
  #
  #   i2clink = "transactional"  one call per bus event straight to the device.
  #                              Faster, and indistinguishable to firmware that
  #                              only drives the peripheral.
  #
  # **A transfer costs the same virtual time either way** -- `bus::i2c` fixes
  # the half-period count of a START, a byte and a STOP, and `CCR` decides what
  # a half period lasts -- so firmware that polls `SR1` sees one timeline.
  param i2clink = "wired"
  param i2cbus  = "i2c1"

  # How much flash the part has: 1 MiB on the -VG suffix, 512 KiB on a -VE.
  param flash = 1M

  # The three RAM banks, separately because they are separately reachable:
  # SRAM1 and SRAM2 are contiguous and on the bus matrix, while CCM is
  # core-coupled and **not reachable by DMA** — a distinction that costs a
  # driver author an afternoon and is a property of the map, so it is modelled
  # as three regions rather than one.
  param sram1 = 112K
  param sram2 = 16K
  param ccm   = 64K

  #
  # The **I2C v1** block: the one an F1, F2, F4 or L1 carries, with `CR1`/`CR2`,
  # `OAR1`, `DR`, `SR1`/`SR2` and `CCR`. An F0, F3, F7 or H7 has a completely
  # different peripheral of the same name, with `TIMINGR`, `ISR` and `ICR`, and
  # firmware for one does not run on the other -- so it is a separate class
  # rather than a property. `src/dev/stm32/i2c.rs` sets out the fork.
  #
  # On APB1, which the clock tree puts at SYSCLK/4 = 42 MHz (RM0090 7.2). The
  # peripheral's `CR2.FREQ` field is firmware telling the hardware that number;
  # the number the *emulator* acts on is this clock, which is why `FREQ` is
  # register storage and this line is not.
  object i2c1 "st.i2c" {
    clock = hse * 21 / 4
    link  = i2clink
    bus   = i2cbus
  }

  # -- the EEPROM, which the Discovery board does not have -------------------
  #
  # **The one part in this file that is not on the real hardware**, and it is
  # here for the reason `machines/spi-panel.machine` exists at all: a bus with
  # nothing on it proves nothing. An AT24C02 on PB6/PB7 is what a person wires
  # to the Discovery board's header when they want to try I2C, and it is the
  # smallest device that exercises addressing, the direction bit, the per-byte
  # acknowledge, the repeated START and a device's own internal timing.
  #
  # `chip = 0` ties A2/A1/A0 low, so it answers 0x50 (datasheet 4.1,
  # Table 4-1). Its clock is a 1 MHz domain derived from the same crystal, so
  # `write-ticks` -- the internally self-timed write cycle of datasheet 5.4 --
  # is in microseconds: the default 5000 is the 5 ms maximum the part specifies.
  # An acknowledge-polling driver (5.3) sees exactly that.
  #
  # `wp` is left unwired, and that is *not* an invented level: Table 1-1's note
  # 1 says the pin is internally pulled down when nothing drives it, and 5.5's
  # Table 5-1 makes a grounded `WP` mean no protection. A board that wants
  # hardware write protection wires `eeprom.wp` to a GPIO.
  object eeprom "atmel.at24c" {
    clock = hse / 8
    chip  = 0
    bus   = i2cbus
  }

  # -- where everything is ---------------------------------------------------
  #
  # Every address is a parameter, and every default below is RM0090 Table 1's
  # number. That is not because an F407 can be reconfigured — it cannot, these
  # are wired into the die — but because a *sibling part* moves them, and the
  # point of a parameter here is that deriving an F401 or an F429 file is
  # editing a value rather than hunting through `map` statements for a
  # hexadecimal constant. An override describes a **different part**, not a
  # different configuration of this one.
  param boot-base   = 0x00000000   # the alias BOOT0/BOOT1 select; flash, here
  param flash-base  = 0x08000000
  param ccm-base    = 0x10000000
  param sram-base   = 0x20000000
  param apb1-base   = 0x40000000
  param apb2-base   = 0x40010000
  param ahb1-base   = 0x40020000

  # Peripheral offsets from their bus's base. An STM32 peripheral is allotted a
  # kilobyte, so the GPIO ports are 0x400 apart and the arithmetic below says so
  # rather than repeating six constants that must agree.
  param usart2-off  = 0x4400       # APB1 + 0x4400
  param i2c1-off    = 0x5400       # APB1 + 0x5400
  param wwdg-off    = 0x2c00       # APB1 + 0x2c00
  param iwdg-off    = 0x3000       # APB1 + 0x3000
  param pwr-off     = 0x7000       # APB1 + 0x7000
  param syscfg-off  = 0x3800       # APB2 + 0x3800
  param exti-off    = 0x3c00       # APB2 + 0x3c00
  param gpio-stride = 0x400
  param crc-off     = 0x3000       # AHB1 + 0x3000
  param rcc-off     = 0x3800       # AHB1 + 0x3800
  param dma-off     = 0x6000       # AHB1 + 0x6000, and DMA2 a kilobyte above
  param dma-stride  = 0x400

  # The timers come in three runs of *consecutive* kilobytes, so one stride and
  # three bases place all fourteen: TIM2-TIM7 and then TIM12-TIM14 from the
  # bottom of APB1, nine in a row with no hole between them; TIM1 and TIM8 from
  # the bottom of APB2; TIM9, TIM10 and TIM11 at APB2 + 0x4000. The run being
  # unbroken is why the arithmetic below counts rather than naming each one —
  # and why the jump from TIM7 to TIM12 at index 6 is worth reading twice.
  param tim-stride  = 0x400
  param tim9-off    = 0x4000       # APB2 + 0x4000: TIM9, TIM10, TIM11

  # -- crystals --------------------------------------------------------------
  #
  # The clock tree in outline. A Discovery board hangs an 8 MHz crystal on HSE
  # and the PLL multiplies it to 168 MHz — RM0090 §7.2, with M=8, N=336, P=2,
  # which is 8 MHz / 8 * 336 / 2. Written as an exact ratio of the crystal
  # rather than as a frequency, because that is what keeps it exact: whatever
  # HSE turns out to be, the core runs at 21 times it (ROADMAP.md §4.2).
  #
  # What is *not* here: HSI, the PLL's own registers, and the AHB/APB
  # prescalers. Those are RCC's, and `st.rcc` computes every one of them as an
  # exact rational and publishes it — but it drives no domain, so a guest that
  # reprograms the PLL changes what RCC reports and changes no rate below. See
  # the header. The ratios here are what a configured F407 runs at.
  osc hse = 8000000 Hz

  # The low-speed internal RC, which is the only thing the independent watchdog
  # counts — that is what makes it independent: it is not on the APB and
  # reprogramming the PLL does not move it. RM0090 §7.2.5 gives it as 32 kHz
  # typical, and DS8626's Table 39 as 17–47 kHz across the full range, which is
  # an uncalibrated RC and why firmware that needs a real timeout uses the LSE.
  # The typical figure is what the manual's own timeout table is computed from.
  osc lsi = 32000 Hz

  # The USART's own can. One tick of a USART's clock domain is one *character*
  # time, so this is divided down to the character rate rather than to the baud
  # rate: 115200 baud, 8N1, is ten bit times per byte, so 11520 bytes a second.
  # 1.8432 MHz is the classic UART crystal and 1843200 / 160 is exactly that,
  # which is why the number is written as a ratio and not as 11520 Hz.
  #
  # In practice this is a *ceiling* rather than the rate. The guest has to write
  # the next byte between the ticks that move them, so a byte costs one
  # scheduler quantum whenever the quantum is longer than a character time —
  # which it is here, and is on `riscv-virt`'s 16550 for the same reason. A
  # slower crystal would throttle the port; a faster one would not speed it up.
  osc uartclk = 1843200 Hz

  # -- the buses -------------------------------------------------------------
  #
  # **Two** 32-bit spaces, because an F4 has two views of its own memory and the
  # difference is the one that costs a driver author an afternoon.
  #
  # `mem` is the core's view: everything the part decodes, CCM included.
  # `dmabus` is what a master on the bus matrix sees (RM0090 §2.1, Figure 1),
  # and **CCM is not in it**. Core-coupled memory hangs off the Cortex-M4's own
  # D-bus and no DMA controller reaches it; a stream programmed at 0x10000000
  # takes a bus error and raises `TEIF`, which is what the silicon does and what
  # the driver author is trying to find out. A comment saying "no DMA reaches
  # it" on a single-space board was not a mechanism — `st.dma` masters whatever
  # space its object names, so the way to say it is to give the controllers a
  # space without CCM in it.
  #
  # What `dmabus` is *not* is a faithful bus matrix. The real one is per master:
  # DMA1's memory port and DMA2's differ over flash and over which peripheral
  # buses they reach, and Ethernet and USB HS have ports of their own. Modelling
  # that means a space per master, and this board models the one distinction
  # that changes whether a driver works. The flash mapping here is `r--` rather
  # than `r-x`: nothing fetches instructions through a DMA controller.
  #
  # `unassigned` is left at its default of `fault` on both, so an access to a
  # hole raises a fault the guest can see rather than a plausible-looking zero
  # — a Cortex-M4 has a BusFault handler, and hiding the abort would hide our
  # own memory-map bugs with it.
  space mem    { width = 32 }
  space dmabus { width = 32 }

  # -- the core --------------------------------------------------------------
  #
  # A Cortex-M4 with the DSP extension and an eight-region MPU, and **four**
  # NVIC priority bits, which is what an F4 implements (RM0090 §10.1.2, "4 bits
  # of interrupt priority are used"). CMSIS discovers the number by writing
  # 0xFF to a priority register and reading back which bits stuck, so getting it
  # wrong changes what a guest's `NVIC_SetPriority` does — it is modelled rather
  # than assumed.
  #
  # **The FPU is present.** `part = "cortex-m4f"` is a Cortex-M4 with FPv4-SP-D16
  # — the unit an F407 actually has, and the one firmware built
  # `-mfloat-abi=hard` needs from its first function call, because GCC uses
  # `s0`–`s31` as scratch even in code that computes no floats. Single precision
  # only: `MVFR0.D_Precision` reads zero, as it does on the part, so a `VADD.F64`
  # is UNDEFINED rather than slow. `part = "cortex-m4"` models the same silicon
  # with the option left out — `CPACR` reads zero and a `VMOV` is a NOCP
  # UsageFault — and is what this board said before the unit landed.
  object cpu "cpu.arm.v7m" {
    clock         = hse * 21
    space         = mem
    part          = "cortex-m4f"
    priority-bits = 4
  }

  # -- memory ----------------------------------------------------------------
  #
  # `image` names a media slot: whoever runs the machine binds the bytes
  # (`--media firmware=fw.bin`), and nothing below host/ opens a file.
  object rom "rom" { size = flash, image = "firmware" }

  object ram1   "ram" { size = sram1 }
  object ram2   "ram" { size = sram2 }
  object ccmram "ram" { size = ccm }

  # -- the clock tree and the power controller -------------------------------
  #
  # `hse` here is the rate of the crystal RCC is told about, which has to agree
  # with the `osc hse` above because they are the same can: the oscillator is
  # what the emulator's domains are derived from and this number is what RCC
  # runs `PLLCFGR`'s M, N, P and Q over. They are written twice because nothing
  # in the machine language lets a property read an oscillator's rate, and the
  # duplication is better than a device that guesses.
  #
  # `ready-delay` is left at its default of sixteen ticks of this device's clock
  # domain — two microseconds of the 8 MHz can. A real HSE takes milliseconds
  # to start and a real PLL microseconds to lock; a board that wants the
  # crystal's figure writes the number and gets it, and firmware that polls a
  # ready bit cannot tell the difference except in how much virtual time it
  # burns. The default is short because nothing here is measuring startup.
  object rcc "st.rcc" {
    clock   = hse
    variant = "f4"
    hse     = 8000000
  }

  # `PWR_CR.DBP` is the only thing on this board with a consumer: it is what
  # unlocks RCC's `BDCR`, and a firmware that configures the RTC writes it
  # first. `variant = "f4"` is the plain F405/F407 block — `CR` and `CSR` and
  # nothing else. An F427/F429 is `"f42x"`, which adds the over-drive bits.
  object pwr "st.pwr" {
    clock   = hse
    variant = "f4"
  }

  # -- the timers ------------------------------------------------------------
  #
  # Fourteen of them, one class. `variant` picks the feature subset: `basic` is
  # TIM6/TIM7, a counter and an update interrupt and nothing else; `advanced` is
  # TIM1/TIM8, which add `RCR`, `BDTR.MOE`, the complementary outputs and a
  # *split* interrupt output; everything else is `general`. `width = 32` is
  # TIM2 and TIM5, which are the only two 32-bit counters on an F4, and
  # `channels` is what the instance bonds — four on TIM1–TIM5 and TIM8, two on
  # TIM9 and TIM12, one on TIM10, TIM11, TIM13 and TIM14 (RM0090 §17–§19).
  #
  # The clock is **`CK_INT`, not the APB bus clock**, and on an F4 the two are
  # not the same number: RM0090 §7.2 says that when an APB prescaler is other
  # than 1 the timer clocks are *twice* the APB clock. This board runs the
  # documented 168 MHz configuration — HPRE = 1, PPRE1 = 4, PPRE2 = 2 — so
  # PCLK1 is 42 MHz and an APB1 timer counts at 84 MHz, PCLK2 is 84 MHz and an
  # APB2 timer counts at 168 MHz. The doubling is applied here rather than in
  # the device, because which half of that rule applies is a property of how the
  # board programmed RCC and the device cannot see it.
  object tim1  "st.tim" { clock = hse * 21,     variant = "advanced", channels = 4 }
  object tim2  "st.tim" { clock = hse * 21 / 2, variant = "general",  channels = 4, width = 32 }
  object tim3  "st.tim" { clock = hse * 21 / 2, variant = "general",  channels = 4 }
  object tim4  "st.tim" { clock = hse * 21 / 2, variant = "general",  channels = 4 }
  object tim5  "st.tim" { clock = hse * 21 / 2, variant = "general",  channels = 4, width = 32 }
  object tim6  "st.tim" { clock = hse * 21 / 2, variant = "basic" }
  object tim7  "st.tim" { clock = hse * 21 / 2, variant = "basic" }
  object tim8  "st.tim" { clock = hse * 21,     variant = "advanced", channels = 4 }
  object tim9  "st.tim" { clock = hse * 21,     variant = "general",  channels = 2 }
  object tim10 "st.tim" { clock = hse * 21,     variant = "general",  channels = 1 }
  object tim11 "st.tim" { clock = hse * 21,     variant = "general",  channels = 1 }
  object tim12 "st.tim" { clock = hse * 21 / 2, variant = "general",  channels = 2 }
  object tim13 "st.tim" { clock = hse * 21 / 2, variant = "general",  channels = 1 }
  object tim14 "st.tim" { clock = hse * 21 / 2, variant = "general",  channels = 1 }

  # -- the DMA controllers ---------------------------------------------------
  #
  # Two, both the stream-based face (RM0090 §10): eight streams each, `LISR`
  # and `HISR`, `SxCR` and the rest. `space = dmabus` is the load-bearing word
  # — see the note on the two spaces above for why CCM is not in it.
  #
  # One tick of the clock domain is one beat, so this is a beat every four AHB
  # cycles. That is a rate rather than a figure from the manual: the real
  # controller arbitrates for the matrix and takes a variable number of cycles
  # per beat, and what matters for firmware is that a transfer *takes time at
  # all* rather than completing inside the store that sets `EN`.
  #
  # **Nothing drives a request line.** `req0`–`req7` are inputs and no
  # peripheral on this board has a request output to wire to them (neither
  # `st.usart` nor `st.i2c` publishes one), so the streams here serve
  # memory-to-memory transfers and nothing else. The request matrix of RM0090
  # Table 43 is a fact about the part and belongs in this file, exactly as a
  # vector number does; it goes in when there is a peripheral that can drive one.
  object dma1 "st.dma" { clock = hse * 21 / 4, space = dmabus, variant = "stream" }
  object dma2 "st.dma" { clock = hse * 21 / 4, space = dmabus, variant = "stream" }

  # -- the external-interrupt path -------------------------------------------
  #
  # Two objects, because on the die it is two blocks: `SYSCFG`'s `EXTICR1`–
  # `EXTICR4` choose *which port's* pin n drives EXTI line n, and `EXTI` decides
  # what an edge on that line does. `ports = 8` is A through H, two letters
  # more than this package bonds — the count is from A and GPIOH is port index
  # 7, so F and G exist in the mux with nothing wired to them.
  #
  # `lines = 23` is the F4's count (RM0090 §12.2.5): 0–15 from the GPIO mux,
  # then 16 PVD, 17 RTC alarm, 18 OTG FS wakeup, 19 Ethernet wakeup, 20 OTG HS
  # wakeup, 21 tamper/timestamp and 22 RTC wakeup. **Lines 16–22 are undriven
  # here**, because none of those peripherals is on this board and `st.pwr`
  # publishes no PVD output; `SWIER` still raises them, which is how a test
  # exercises a line with no source.
  object syscfg "st.syscfg" { variant = "f4", ports = 8 }
  object exti   "st.exti"   { lines = 23 }

  # -- the CRC unit ----------------------------------------------------------
  #
  # `variant = "f4"` is the fixed-polynomial block: CRC-32/MPEG-2, word writes
  # only, and no `POL` or `INIT` register. The programmable one that the
  # F0/F3/F7/L4 carry is `"v2"` and is a different register file, which is why
  # it is a property and not an assumption.
  object crc "st.crc" { variant = "f4" }

  # -- the watchdogs ---------------------------------------------------------
  #
  # The independent one counts the LSI and `window = false` because an F4 has no
  # `WINR`; the window one counts PCLK1 and `wdgtb-shift = 7` puts `WDGTB` at
  # bits [8:7], which is where an F4 has it (an L4 moved it to [12:11]).
  #
  # Both drive `reset`, and both are wired to the core's reset input below.
  object iwdg "st.iwdg" { clock = lsi,           window = false }
  object wwdg "st.wwdg" { clock = hse * 21 / 4,  wdgtb-shift = 7 }

  # -- the GPIO ports --------------------------------------------------------
  #
  # One class, six instances, differing only in their reset values — which are
  # not decorative: `GPIOA` comes up with PA13–PA15 already in alternate
  # function and pulled, because that is the SWD/JTAG debug port, and `GPIOB`
  # the same for PB3/PB4 (RM0090 §8.4.1, §8.4.3, §8.4.4). Firmware that reads
  # `MODER` back to decide what to touch sees the real thing.
  object gpioa "st.gpio" { moder-reset = 0xa8000000, pupdr-reset = 0x64000000 }
  object gpiob "st.gpio" { moder-reset = 0x00000280, ospeedr-reset = 0x000000c0, pupdr-reset = 0x00000100 }
  object gpioc "st.gpio" { }
  object gpiod "st.gpio" { }
  object gpioe "st.gpio" { }
  object gpioh "st.gpio" { }

  # -- USART2 ----------------------------------------------------------------
  #
  # On PA2 (TX) and PA3 (RX) through AF7, which is the pair a Discovery board's
  # ST-LINK virtual COM port lands on. `variant = "f4"` is the default and is
  # written out because the register map is the one thing about this peripheral
  # that is not portable between families: an F7 or an H7 puts `ISR` at +0x1c
  # and clears flags through `ICR`, and firmware for one does not run on the
  # other.
  object usart2 "st.usart" {
    clock   = uartclk / 160
    port    = console
    variant = "f4"
  }

  # -- the memory map --------------------------------------------------------
  #
  # Every base is a `param` from the top of the file; see the note there for why
  # a fixed part still parameterises its own map.
  #
  # `perms = "r-x"` on the flash: the part's flash is not writable through the
  # AHB — a program to it goes through the flash interface's key sequence, which
  # this board does not model — so a stray `STR` into it is a BusFault rather
  # than a silently modified program. That is a property of the *decode* in
  # front of the chip and so belongs on the mapping (`core::space`).
  #
  # It is mapped twice on purpose. 0x08000000 is where flash lives; 0x00000000
  # is the **boot alias**, which is what the BOOT0/BOOT1 pins select and what
  # the core fetches its initial `SP` and `PC` from out of reset. Modelling the
  # pins would mean an RCC-shaped device; a board that boots from flash is the
  # overwhelmingly common case and this file states which one it is.
  map mem boot-base            size flash = rom { perms = "r-x" }
  map mem flash-base           size flash = rom { perms = "r-x" }
  map mem ccm-base             size ccm   = ccmram   # and only in `mem`
  map mem sram-base            size sram1 = ram1
  map mem sram-base + sram1    size sram2 = ram2     # SRAM2 abuts SRAM1

  # Then the peripherals, bus by bus. Each register block is mapped at its own
  # base and at its own size: a peripheral decodes the low bytes of its kilobyte
  # and the rest of the kilobyte is a **hole** rather than a mirror — which is
  # what the part does, and what makes a wrong offset a fault instead of a
  # plausible answer. The one place that is generous is a timer: `st.tim`
  # decodes one 0x50 window whatever the variant, so TIM6 and TIM7 answer above
  # the `ARR` their registers stop at.
  #
  # APB1 from the bottom: the nine timers it carries, the two watchdogs, the
  # USART, I2C1 and the power controller.
  map mem apb1-base + tim-stride * 0 size 0x50 = tim2
  map mem apb1-base + tim-stride * 1 size 0x50 = tim3
  map mem apb1-base + tim-stride * 2 size 0x50 = tim4
  map mem apb1-base + tim-stride * 3 size 0x50 = tim5
  map mem apb1-base + tim-stride * 4 size 0x50 = tim6
  map mem apb1-base + tim-stride * 5 size 0x50 = tim7
  map mem apb1-base + tim-stride * 6 size 0x50 = tim12
  map mem apb1-base + tim-stride * 7 size 0x50 = tim13
  map mem apb1-base + tim-stride * 8 size 0x50 = tim14
  map mem apb1-base + wwdg-off   size 0x0c = wwdg
  map mem apb1-base + iwdg-off   size 0x14 = iwdg
  map mem apb1-base + usart2-off size 0x2c = usart2
  map mem apb1-base + i2c1-off   size 0x28 = i2c1
  map mem apb1-base + pwr-off    size 0x08 = pwr

  # APB2: the two advanced timers at the bottom, then SYSCFG and EXTI, then the
  # TIM9/TIM10/TIM11 run four kilobytes up.
  map mem apb2-base + tim-stride * 0 size 0x50 = tim1
  map mem apb2-base + tim-stride * 1 size 0x50 = tim8
  map mem apb2-base + syscfg-off size 0x24 = syscfg
  map mem apb2-base + exti-off   size 0x18 = exti
  map mem apb2-base + tim9-off + tim-stride * 0 size 0x50 = tim9
  map mem apb2-base + tim9-off + tim-stride * 1 size 0x50 = tim10
  map mem apb2-base + tim9-off + tim-stride * 2 size 0x50 = tim11

  # AHB1. The six GPIO ports the LQFP100 bonds, at A + n * 0x400: A, B, C, D, E,
  # then H, which is 7. F, G and I exist on the die and have no pins in this
  # package. Then CRC, RCC, and the two DMA controllers.
  map mem ahb1-base + gpio-stride * 0 size 0x28 = gpioa
  map mem ahb1-base + gpio-stride * 1 size 0x28 = gpiob
  map mem ahb1-base + gpio-stride * 2 size 0x28 = gpioc
  map mem ahb1-base + gpio-stride * 3 size 0x28 = gpiod
  map mem ahb1-base + gpio-stride * 4 size 0x28 = gpioe
  map mem ahb1-base + gpio-stride * 7 size 0x28 = gpioh
  map mem ahb1-base + crc-off size 0x0c = crc
  map mem ahb1-base + rcc-off size 0x90 = rcc
  map mem ahb1-base + dma-off + dma-stride * 0 size 0xd0 = dma1
  map mem ahb1-base + dma-off + dma-stride * 1 size 0xd0 = dma2

  # -- the same map as a bus master sees it ----------------------------------
  #
  # `dmabus`, which is what `dma1` and `dma2` transfer across. It is `mem`
  # minus two things: **CCM**, which no master on the matrix reaches, and the
  # boot alias, which exists for the core's first fetch and is not a window
  # anything DMAs through. Flash is `r--` here rather than `r-x` because nothing
  # executes out of a DMA controller.
  #
  # The DMA controllers' own register blocks are deliberately absent: a stream
  # that reprograms a stream is not a transfer any board wants modelled, and
  # leaving them out makes it a fault rather than a surprise.
  map dmabus flash-base           size flash = rom { perms = "r--" }
  map dmabus sram-base            size sram1 = ram1
  map dmabus sram-base + sram1    size sram2 = ram2
  map dmabus apb1-base + tim-stride * 0 size 0x50 = tim2
  map dmabus apb1-base + tim-stride * 1 size 0x50 = tim3
  map dmabus apb1-base + tim-stride * 2 size 0x50 = tim4
  map dmabus apb1-base + tim-stride * 3 size 0x50 = tim5
  map dmabus apb1-base + tim-stride * 4 size 0x50 = tim6
  map dmabus apb1-base + tim-stride * 5 size 0x50 = tim7
  map dmabus apb1-base + tim-stride * 6 size 0x50 = tim12
  map dmabus apb1-base + tim-stride * 7 size 0x50 = tim13
  map dmabus apb1-base + tim-stride * 8 size 0x50 = tim14
  map dmabus apb1-base + wwdg-off   size 0x0c = wwdg
  map dmabus apb1-base + iwdg-off   size 0x14 = iwdg
  map dmabus apb1-base + usart2-off size 0x2c = usart2
  map dmabus apb1-base + i2c1-off   size 0x28 = i2c1
  map dmabus apb1-base + pwr-off    size 0x08 = pwr
  map dmabus apb2-base + tim-stride * 0 size 0x50 = tim1
  map dmabus apb2-base + tim-stride * 1 size 0x50 = tim8
  map dmabus apb2-base + syscfg-off size 0x24 = syscfg
  map dmabus apb2-base + exti-off   size 0x18 = exti
  map dmabus apb2-base + tim9-off + tim-stride * 0 size 0x50 = tim9
  map dmabus apb2-base + tim9-off + tim-stride * 1 size 0x50 = tim10
  map dmabus apb2-base + tim9-off + tim-stride * 2 size 0x50 = tim11
  map dmabus ahb1-base + gpio-stride * 0 size 0x28 = gpioa
  map dmabus ahb1-base + gpio-stride * 1 size 0x28 = gpiob
  map dmabus ahb1-base + gpio-stride * 2 size 0x28 = gpioc
  map dmabus ahb1-base + gpio-stride * 3 size 0x28 = gpiod
  map dmabus ahb1-base + gpio-stride * 4 size 0x28 = gpioe
  map dmabus ahb1-base + gpio-stride * 7 size 0x28 = gpioh
  map dmabus ahb1-base + crc-off size 0x0c = crc
  map dmabus ahb1-base + rcc-off size 0x90 = rcc

  # -- interrupts ------------------------------------------------------------
  #
  # The reason this board exists: no controller between the peripheral and the
  # core, and every vector-table position written once, here. Each number below
  # is a **position in RM0090 Table 62** and is named in the comment beside it,
  # because a wrong one does not fail — it silently runs somebody else's
  # handler, and no amount of testing the device finds that.
  #
  # Two properties of Table 62 shape the lines below.
  #
  # * **A position can have more than one source.** TIM1's update event and
  #   TIM9's only interrupt share position 25; EXTI lines 5 through 9 share 23
  #   and 10 through 15 share 40. Those are wired-OR on the die and are a fan-in
  #   here: several `wire` statements naming one `cpu.irq{n}`, which
  #   `core::wire` resolves. They are written out one per source, which is what
  #   `ROADMAP.md` §5 asks for.
  # * **A source can have more than one position.** An advanced timer splits
  #   its interrupt four ways — break, update, trigger/commutation, and
  #   capture/compare — and `st.tim` publishes two of those four, `irq-up` and
  #   `irq-cc`. Break and trigger/commutation have no pin because the model has
  #   no `BDTR` break input and no commutation event (`src/dev/stm32/tim.rs`,
  #   "What is modelled, and what is not"), so positions 24 and 26 are driven by
  #   TIM9 and TIM11 alone and 43 and 45 by TIM12 and TIM14 alone. When the
  #   break input lands, it gets a line here and nothing else changes.
  #
  # What is *not* wired, and why, so the gaps are legible rather than lost:
  # position 4 (FLASH) and 5 (RCC) — `st.rcc`'s `CIR` is storage and raises no
  # interrupt; 1 (PVD) — `st.pwr` publishes no PVD output; 18–22 (ADC, CAN1)
  # and everything above 47 that is not a timer — no such device on this board.
  # The independent watchdog has no vector at all on any STM32: expiry is a
  # reset, which is why `iwdg` appears under the reset wiring and not here.

  # WWDG's early-wakeup interrupt is **position 0**, the first entry in Table 62
  # and the lowest-numbered external interrupt an STM32F4 has. It fires one
  # decrement before the reset, which is the last chance firmware has to save
  # anything.
  wire wwdg.ewi -> cpu.irq0

  # EXTI. Lines 0-4 have a vector each at positions 6-10; lines 5-9 share
  # position 23 (`EXTI9_5`) and lines 10-15 share 40 (`EXTI15_10`). A handler
  # for either of the grouped ones reads `PR` to find out which line it was,
  # which is why one core pin with five or six sources on it is the faithful
  # model and not a shortcut.
  wire exti.irq0  -> cpu.irq6     # EXTI0
  wire exti.irq1  -> cpu.irq7     # EXTI1
  wire exti.irq2  -> cpu.irq8     # EXTI2
  wire exti.irq3  -> cpu.irq9     # EXTI3
  wire exti.irq4  -> cpu.irq10    # EXTI4
  wire exti.irq5  -> cpu.irq23    # EXTI9_5
  wire exti.irq6  -> cpu.irq23    # EXTI9_5
  wire exti.irq7  -> cpu.irq23    # EXTI9_5
  wire exti.irq8  -> cpu.irq23    # EXTI9_5
  wire exti.irq9  -> cpu.irq23    # EXTI9_5
  wire exti.irq10 -> cpu.irq40    # EXTI15_10
  wire exti.irq11 -> cpu.irq40    # EXTI15_10
  wire exti.irq12 -> cpu.irq40    # EXTI15_10
  wire exti.irq13 -> cpu.irq40    # EXTI15_10
  wire exti.irq14 -> cpu.irq40    # EXTI15_10
  wire exti.irq15 -> cpu.irq40    # EXTI15_10

  # The timers. The four advanced-timer positions are shared with TIM9-TIM11
  # (for TIM1) and TIM12-TIM14 (for TIM8), which is what their names in Table 62
  # say: `TIM1_BRK_TIM9`, `TIM1_UP_TIM10`, `TIM1_TRG_COM_TIM11`, `TIM1_CC`.
  wire tim9.irq     -> cpu.irq24   # TIM1_BRK_TIM9 (TIM1's break has no pin)
  wire tim1.irq-up  -> cpu.irq25   # TIM1_UP_TIM10
  wire tim10.irq    -> cpu.irq25   # TIM1_UP_TIM10
  wire tim11.irq    -> cpu.irq26   # TIM1_TRG_COM_TIM11 (no commutation pin)
  wire tim1.irq-cc  -> cpu.irq27   # TIM1_CC
  wire tim2.irq     -> cpu.irq28   # TIM2
  wire tim3.irq     -> cpu.irq29   # TIM3
  wire tim4.irq     -> cpu.irq30   # TIM4
  wire tim12.irq    -> cpu.irq43   # TIM8_BRK_TIM12 (TIM8's break has no pin)
  wire tim8.irq-up  -> cpu.irq44   # TIM8_UP_TIM13
  wire tim13.irq    -> cpu.irq44   # TIM8_UP_TIM13
  wire tim14.irq    -> cpu.irq45   # TIM8_TRG_COM_TIM14 (no commutation pin)
  wire tim8.irq-cc  -> cpu.irq46   # TIM8_CC
  wire tim5.irq     -> cpu.irq50   # TIM5
  wire tim6.irq     -> cpu.irq54   # TIM6_DAC — shared with the DAC's underrun
  wire tim7.irq     -> cpu.irq55   # TIM7

  # The DMA controllers, one vector per stream. Neither controller's streams are
  # contiguous in the table: DMA1's streams 0-6 are at 11-17 and its **stream 7
  # is at 47**, and DMA2's streams 0-4 are at 56-60 with 5, 6 and 7 at 68, 69
  # and 70. Those two discontinuities are the easiest thing on this whole part
  # to get wrong, and a board that assumed the obvious run would put six
  # transfers' completions on six other peripherals' handlers.
  wire dma1.irq0 -> cpu.irq11    # DMA1_Stream0
  wire dma1.irq1 -> cpu.irq12    # DMA1_Stream1
  wire dma1.irq2 -> cpu.irq13    # DMA1_Stream2
  wire dma1.irq3 -> cpu.irq14    # DMA1_Stream3
  wire dma1.irq4 -> cpu.irq15    # DMA1_Stream4
  wire dma1.irq5 -> cpu.irq16    # DMA1_Stream5
  wire dma1.irq6 -> cpu.irq17    # DMA1_Stream6
  wire dma1.irq7 -> cpu.irq47    # DMA1_Stream7
  wire dma2.irq0 -> cpu.irq56    # DMA2_Stream0
  wire dma2.irq1 -> cpu.irq57    # DMA2_Stream1
  wire dma2.irq2 -> cpu.irq58    # DMA2_Stream2
  wire dma2.irq3 -> cpu.irq59    # DMA2_Stream3
  wire dma2.irq4 -> cpu.irq60    # DMA2_Stream4
  wire dma2.irq5 -> cpu.irq68    # DMA2_Stream5
  wire dma2.irq6 -> cpu.irq69    # DMA2_Stream6
  wire dma2.irq7 -> cpu.irq70    # DMA2_Stream7

  wire usart2.irq -> cpu.irq38    # USART2 (USART1 is 37, USART3 39)

  # -- resets ----------------------------------------------------------------
  #
  # Either watchdog expiring pulses `reset`, and a watchdog reset on the part is
  # a system reset: the core restarts and `RCC_CSR` remembers which of the two
  # did it, because the flag is RCC's state and not the watchdog's. So each
  # pulse goes two places — the core's reset input, and the matching cause pin
  # on RCC. That is the whole of the difference between a board that reboots and
  # a board whose firmware can tell you *why* it rebooted.
  #
  # `cpu.reset` resets the core and not the peripherals, which is narrower than
  # the part: a real `NRST` clears every block on the die. Nothing here relies
  # on the difference, and widening it needs a machine-level reset sink that
  # does not exist yet.
  wire iwdg.reset -> cpu.reset
  wire iwdg.reset -> rcc.iwdgrst
  wire wwdg.reset -> cpu.reset
  wire wwdg.reset -> rcc.wwdgrst

  # -- the backup domain -----------------------------------------------------
  #
  # `PWR_CR.DBP` is a level on a net rather than a handle, because that is what
  # it is on the die: it is what makes RCC's `BDCR` writable, and a firmware
  # that configures the RTC or the LSE sets it first and clears it after.
  wire pwr.dbp -> rcc.dbp

  # -- the pins --------------------------------------------------------------
  #
  # PD12–PD15 are the Discovery board's four LEDs — green, orange, red, blue.
  # They are outputs of the port and this board has nothing to wire them to, so
  # they are left unwired and a test reads them through `GPIOD`'s `IDR`, which
  # is what a scope on the pin would see. A board with a panel or an LED matrix
  # wires `gpiod.p12` into it.
  #
  # USART2's own pins are likewise unwired, and that is a *modelling* decision
  # rather than an omission: the USART's data path is the character seam in
  # `host::chardev`, not a bit stream on a wire, so `gpioa.af2` and `gpioa.af3`
  # have nothing to carry. The GPIO's alternate-function inputs exist for the
  # peripherals whose pin genuinely is a level — an SPI clock, a chip select, a
  # timer's compare output.

  # -- the external-interrupt mux --------------------------------------------
  #
  # Three hops, and the middle one is the mux: the port drives its pin, SYSCFG's
  # `EXTICR` decides which port's pin *n* is what EXTI line *n* sees, and EXTI
  # decides what an edge there does. Only the first hop is per-pin wiring, and
  # it is written out below because a machine file has no loops — which is the
  # honest cost of saying, once, that every bonded pin on this package can be an
  # interrupt source. A port whose pin is not selected by `EXTICR` drives a
  # level nothing looks at, exactly as on the die.
  #
  # GPIOH is two lines rather than sixteen: the LQFP100 bonds PH0 and PH1 (the
  # OSC_IN/OSC_OUT pair) and nothing else of port H, so there are no other pins
  # to be an interrupt source.
  wire gpioa.p0  -> syscfg.pa0
  wire gpioa.p1  -> syscfg.pa1
  wire gpioa.p2  -> syscfg.pa2
  wire gpioa.p3  -> syscfg.pa3
  wire gpioa.p4  -> syscfg.pa4
  wire gpioa.p5  -> syscfg.pa5
  wire gpioa.p6  -> syscfg.pa6
  wire gpioa.p7  -> syscfg.pa7
  wire gpioa.p8  -> syscfg.pa8
  wire gpioa.p9  -> syscfg.pa9
  wire gpioa.p10 -> syscfg.pa10
  wire gpioa.p11 -> syscfg.pa11
  wire gpioa.p12 -> syscfg.pa12
  wire gpioa.p13 -> syscfg.pa13
  wire gpioa.p14 -> syscfg.pa14
  wire gpioa.p15 -> syscfg.pa15
  wire gpiob.p0  -> syscfg.pb0
  wire gpiob.p1  -> syscfg.pb1
  wire gpiob.p2  -> syscfg.pb2
  wire gpiob.p3  -> syscfg.pb3
  wire gpiob.p4  -> syscfg.pb4
  wire gpiob.p5  -> syscfg.pb5
  wire gpiob.p6  -> syscfg.pb6
  wire gpiob.p7  -> syscfg.pb7
  wire gpiob.p8  -> syscfg.pb8
  wire gpiob.p9  -> syscfg.pb9
  wire gpiob.p10 -> syscfg.pb10
  wire gpiob.p11 -> syscfg.pb11
  wire gpiob.p12 -> syscfg.pb12
  wire gpiob.p13 -> syscfg.pb13
  wire gpiob.p14 -> syscfg.pb14
  wire gpiob.p15 -> syscfg.pb15
  wire gpioc.p0  -> syscfg.pc0
  wire gpioc.p1  -> syscfg.pc1
  wire gpioc.p2  -> syscfg.pc2
  wire gpioc.p3  -> syscfg.pc3
  wire gpioc.p4  -> syscfg.pc4
  wire gpioc.p5  -> syscfg.pc5
  wire gpioc.p6  -> syscfg.pc6
  wire gpioc.p7  -> syscfg.pc7
  wire gpioc.p8  -> syscfg.pc8
  wire gpioc.p9  -> syscfg.pc9
  wire gpioc.p10 -> syscfg.pc10
  wire gpioc.p11 -> syscfg.pc11
  wire gpioc.p12 -> syscfg.pc12
  wire gpioc.p13 -> syscfg.pc13
  wire gpioc.p14 -> syscfg.pc14
  wire gpioc.p15 -> syscfg.pc15
  wire gpiod.p0  -> syscfg.pd0
  wire gpiod.p1  -> syscfg.pd1
  wire gpiod.p2  -> syscfg.pd2
  wire gpiod.p3  -> syscfg.pd3
  wire gpiod.p4  -> syscfg.pd4
  wire gpiod.p5  -> syscfg.pd5
  wire gpiod.p6  -> syscfg.pd6
  wire gpiod.p7  -> syscfg.pd7
  wire gpiod.p8  -> syscfg.pd8
  wire gpiod.p9  -> syscfg.pd9
  wire gpiod.p10 -> syscfg.pd10
  wire gpiod.p11 -> syscfg.pd11
  wire gpiod.p12 -> syscfg.pd12
  wire gpiod.p13 -> syscfg.pd13
  wire gpiod.p14 -> syscfg.pd14
  wire gpiod.p15 -> syscfg.pd15
  wire gpioe.p0  -> syscfg.pe0
  wire gpioe.p1  -> syscfg.pe1
  wire gpioe.p2  -> syscfg.pe2
  wire gpioe.p3  -> syscfg.pe3
  wire gpioe.p4  -> syscfg.pe4
  wire gpioe.p5  -> syscfg.pe5
  wire gpioe.p6  -> syscfg.pe6
  wire gpioe.p7  -> syscfg.pe7
  wire gpioe.p8  -> syscfg.pe8
  wire gpioe.p9  -> syscfg.pe9
  wire gpioe.p10 -> syscfg.pe10
  wire gpioe.p11 -> syscfg.pe11
  wire gpioe.p12 -> syscfg.pe12
  wire gpioe.p13 -> syscfg.pe13
  wire gpioe.p14 -> syscfg.pe14
  wire gpioe.p15 -> syscfg.pe15
  wire gpioh.p0  -> syscfg.ph0
  wire gpioh.p1  -> syscfg.ph1

  # The mux's sixteen outputs into EXTI's sixteen GPIO lines. Lines 16-22 are
  # the peripheral wakeups (PVD, the RTC, the OTG and Ethernet cores) and are
  # undriven on this board — see the `exti` object above.
  wire syscfg.exti0  -> exti.line0
  wire syscfg.exti1  -> exti.line1
  wire syscfg.exti2  -> exti.line2
  wire syscfg.exti3  -> exti.line3
  wire syscfg.exti4  -> exti.line4
  wire syscfg.exti5  -> exti.line5
  wire syscfg.exti6  -> exti.line6
  wire syscfg.exti7  -> exti.line7
  wire syscfg.exti8  -> exti.line8
  wire syscfg.exti9  -> exti.line9
  wire syscfg.exti10 -> exti.line10
  wire syscfg.exti11 -> exti.line11
  wire syscfg.exti12 -> exti.line12
  wire syscfg.exti13 -> exti.line13
  wire syscfg.exti14 -> exti.line14
  wire syscfg.exti15 -> exti.line15

  # -- I2C: the interrupt pair, then the two open-drain nets -----------------
  #
  # Each of SCL and SDA is *two* statements, because on a board it is one wire
  # every device may pull low; `bus::i2c::wires` resolves that as a wired-AND,
  # so clock stretching and arbitration are levels rather than flags.
  wire i2c1.ev -> cpu.irq31
  wire i2c1.er -> cpu.irq32
  wire i2c1.scl   -> eeprom.scl
  wire eeprom.scl -> i2c1.scl
  wire i2c1.sda   -> eeprom.sda
  wire eeprom.sda -> i2c1.sda
}
